{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1932 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "f742c906b4f51dc1498dd065271821b577a411884c52cb1dbd6d7d12c0470ad1" :edb/db-before-t 1931 :edb/db-after-t 1932 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535244 50 #inst "2026-10-08T20:20:06.052Z" 13194139535244 true] [17592186096302 1100 "Given that $y=x^3+e^x$ for $x=x(t)$, and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=2$ at the point where $x=1$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$." 13194139535244 false] [17592186096302 1100 "Given that $y=x^3+e^x$ for $x=x(t),$ and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=2$ at the point where $x=1,$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}.$" 13194139535244 true] [17592186096302 1145 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x^{3} + e^{x} \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(y) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(x^{3} + e^{x}) \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3} + e^{x}) \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot (3x^{2} + e^{x})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=2\n$$\n\nand $x=1$, and solve for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot (3 \\cdot 1^{2} + e^{1}) \\\\\n2 &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot (3 + e) \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{2}{3 + e}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535244 false] [17592186096302 1145 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{3}+{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}+{e}^{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+{e}^{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (3{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=2$ and $x=1,$ and solve for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (3\\cdot {1}^{2}+{e}^{1}) \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (3+e) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2}{3+e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535244 true] [17592186096303 1100 "Given that $r=\\sin\\theta$ for $\\theta = \\theta(t)$, and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}=\\sqrt{2}$ at the point where $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\theta}{\\textrm d t}$." 13194139535244 false] [17592186096303 1100 "Given that $r=\\sin\\theta$ for $\\theta = \\theta(t),$ and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}=\\sqrt{2}$ at the point where $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\theta}{\\textrm d t}.$" 13194139535244 true] [17592186096303 1145 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\sin θ \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(r) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin θ) \\\\\n\\frac{dr}{dt} &= \\frac{dθ}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dθ}(\\sin θ) \\\\\n\\frac{dr}{dt} &= \\frac{dθ}{dt} \\cdot \\cos θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}=\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nand solve for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\theta}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dr}{dt} &= \\frac{dθ}{dt} \\cdot \\cos θ \\\\\n\\sqrt{2} &= \\frac{dθ}{dt} \\cdot \\cos (\\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n\\sqrt{2} &= \\frac{dθ}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{dθ}{dt} &= \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n\\frac{dθ}{dt} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535244 false] [17592186096303 1145 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ and solve for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\theta}{\\textrm d t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535244 true] [17592186096304 1100 "A particle $P$ is moving along the curve $y=x+e^x$ such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is changing at the rate of $2 \\,\\textrm{cm}/\\textrm{s}$. Find the rate of change of the $y$-coordinate when $x=5\\,\\textrm{cm}$." 13194139535244 false] [17592186096304 1100 "A particle $P$ is moving along the curve $y=x+e^x$ such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is changing at the rate of $2 \\,\\textrm{cm}/\\textrm{s}.$ Find the rate of change of the $y$-coordinate when $x=5\\,\\textrm{cm}.$" 13194139535244 true] [17592186096304 1145 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x + e^{x} \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(y) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(x + e^{x}) \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x + e^{x}) \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot (1 + e^{x})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can substitute the given information\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=2 \\,\\textrm{cm}/\\textrm{s}\n$$\n\nand $x=5$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= 2(1 + e^{5})cm/s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535244 false] [17592186096304 1145 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x+{e}^{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x+{e}^{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (1+{e}^{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can substitute the given information $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=2 \\,\\textrm{cm}/\\textrm{s}$ and $x=5$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2(1+{e}^{5})\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535244 true] [17592186096727 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339629 13194139535244 true] [17592186096727 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339632 13194139535244 true] [17592186096727 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339644 13194139535244 true] [17592186096727 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339651 13194139535244 true] [17592186096728 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339612 13194139535244 true] [17592186096728 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339626 13194139535244 true] [17592186096728 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339635 13194139535244 true] [17592186096728 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339658 13194139535244 true] [17592186096729 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339619 13194139535244 true] [17592186096729 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339638 13194139535244 true] [17592186096729 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339641 13194139535244 true] [17592186096729 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339665 13194139535244 true] [17592186096729 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339672 13194139535244 true] [17592186097172 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535244 true] [17592186097172 1100 "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nGiven that $y = 2x^{3} - 2x$ for $x = x(t)$, and that $\\frac{dx}{dt} = 1$ at the point where $x =-2$, find $\\frac{dy}{dt}$." 13194139535244 false] [17592186097172 1100 "Given that $y=